# Ruby on Rails testing

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic testing rules to Rails
testing idiom. Covers RSpec (the more common choice in current Rails
apps) and notes the Minitest equivalent where it differs meaningfully.
Applies only to `*.rb` files.

## Layout and framework choice

- Match whichever framework the project already uses — `spec/` with
  RSpec (`*_spec.rb`) or `test/` with Minitest (`*_test.rb`); do not
  introduce the other framework into a project that has standardized on
  one.
- Mirror `app/`'s structure under `spec/`/`test/`: a model at
  `app/models/order.rb` gets a spec at `spec/models/order_spec.rb`, a
  job at `app/jobs/charge_job.rb` gets
  `spec/jobs/charge_job_spec.rb`, and so on.

## RSpec conventions

- `describe`/`context`/`it` read as a sentence — `context` for a
  precondition ("when the user is not signed in"), `it` for the
  observable behavior ("redirects to the login page"); avoid vague
  `it "works"` descriptions.
- Use `let`/`let!` for lazily/eagerly built fixtures shared across
  examples in a block instead of repeating setup in every `it`;
  `before` blocks for side effects that must run before each example.
- Request specs (`spec/requests`) exercise a controller action through
  the full stack (routing, before_actions, rendering) and are generally
  preferred over controller specs for new Rails apps; system specs
  (`spec/system`, via Capybara) exercise a full browser flow — pick the
  narrowest layer that actually covers what changed.
- Use FactoryBot (`create`/`build`, `build_stubbed` when persistence
  isn't needed) if the project already has it configured, matching its
  existing factory definitions rather than hand-building fixtures a
  different way.

## Minitest conventions (when the project uses it instead)

- `test "does something" do ... end` inside a `class XyzTest <
  ActiveSupport::TestCase` (or `ActionDispatch::IntegrationTest` for
  request tests); fixtures come from `test/fixtures/*.yml` or the
  project's own factory setup.
- Assertions read `assert_equal expected, actual` (expected first) and
  `assert_predicate`/`assert_raises` over a bare `assert` on a boolean
  expression, for a clearer failure message.

## Database and external boundaries

- Tests run inside a transaction per example (RSpec's
  `use_transactional_fixtures`/Rails' default Minitest behavior) that
  rolls back automatically — do not manually clean up records created
  in a test unless the app has opted out of transactional tests for a
  specific reason (e.g. system specs across threads), in which case
  match whatever cleanup strategy (DatabaseCleaner, truncation) it
  already uses.
- Stub/mock an external HTTP call (a third-party API, a webhook) at the
  boundary (WebMock, VCR cassettes, or the project's existing tooling)
  — a test must not make a real network call.
- Assert an `ActiveJob` was enqueued with `have_enqueued_job`
  (RSpec) / `assert_enqueued_with` (Minitest) rather than asserting on
  its side effects by actually running it, unless the test's purpose is
  specifically to exercise the job's `perform` behavior — in which case
  drain the queue explicitly (`perform_enqueued_jobs`) instead of
  sleeping or polling for it to run.

## Determinism

- Never synchronize with `sleep` to wait for a background job, an
  async callback, or an eventually-consistent read — use
  `perform_enqueued_jobs`, a Capybara-provided wait matcher
  (`have_content` retries automatically), or an explicit polling helper
  with a timeout.
- Freeze time (`travel_to`/`Timecop`, matching whatever the project
  already uses) for any test asserting on a timestamp or a
  time-dependent calculation, instead of asserting against
  `Time.current` computed twice and racing a clock tick.

## Regression discipline

- A bug fix gets a regression test that fails before the fix and passes
  after; new behavior gets new coverage in the same change.
